From 93a2c11ee815e6e429637aa5de14d296dcce7c05 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 19:29:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] code attestation: attest the managed-stub relaunch
 route; quiet start reads the code ledger; quiet-hold --check

The MacBook gateway always runs as the child `venv_sync.relaunch_command` builds
(`python -I -I -c "<relaunch prefix> exec('<managed stub>')"`). `_detect_route` refused it
outright (`relaunched-launch`), so the <=10% idle proof could neither pass nor fail there.

New route `managed-stub-relaunch` (reported under that name), same invariant as the direct
route: every byte of Python that runs before the audit hook is identified byte-exact and
nothing the process says about itself (flags, env, sys.orig_argv) is evidence on its own.
- Accept only `sys.orig_argv == [exe, "-I" x>=1 (nothing else), "-c", src]` with
  `src == relaunch_prefix(core_root, sys.argv) + "exec(" + repr(script) + ")"` byte for byte.
  The prefix text now lives once in stdlib-only `_launch_template.relaunch_prefix`, used by
  both `venv_sync.relaunch_command` and attestation (attestation never imports venv_sync).
- `script` is recovered with `ast.literal_eval` and passes the unchanged managed-stub checks
  (pin marker, reviewed head digest set, byte-exact tail, root).
- Frame chain (verified in a real subprocess): install <- hermes_bootstrap <module> <-
  importlib frames only (the prefix's spec.loader.exec_module) <- the outermost __main__
  <string> frame, whose code must be `_same` as compile(src), at its single line.
- After the hook, the prefix's one exec() of exactly compile(script), from exactly that
  proven frame, is attributed to the trusted launcher (one-shot); any other string exec is
  observed as before (`unrooted-exec`), a different code object latches
  `relaunch-exec-mismatch`.
- Pre-hook prefix imports (runpy, importlib.util, pathlib): a finite seed for this route
  only, each required to be an installed-stdlib source file and reported with its digest.
  The direct route's seed is unchanged.
- HERMES_LAUNCH_CURRENT_HANDOFF trace: `venv_sync.exec_launch` sets it for the exec'd child;
  the child's bootstrap installs attestation (hermes_bootstrap `_install_code_attestation`)
  BEFORE `prepare_launch` -> `_take_handoff` pops it, so a POSIX relaunch always installs with
  it set. It carries a currency verdict (`<verdict>:<pid>:<root>`), never code, so the
  relaunch route accepts it and reports `launch_handoff_env`; the direct route still refuses
  it (`relaunched-launch`).
- Still refused, each by a real-subprocess test: run_module body, run_path body, any other
  interpreter option, argv mismatch, a prefix differing in any byte, extra statements, a
  non-canonical exec literal, a tampered embedded script, a different root, and a process
  that rewrites sys.orig_argv to the genuine relaunch (frame-code).

`test_relaunch_prefix_script_refuses` changes meaning: it pinned the blanket refusal of this
exact launch. It is replaced by the accept test (through an interpreter path containing a
space, `-I -I` like the Mac) plus one refusal test per near-miss above
(tests/hermes_cli/test_code_attestation_relaunch.py).

Gate: before this change nothing in production read the code-attestation ledger into the
quiet gate (bootstrap installed it; `quiet-observe-start` never consulted it), so even an
unsupported route did not refuse start. `quiet_control` now captures the bootstrap-installed
ledger with the registry: start refuses `code_attestation_missing` (coverage pending) and any
ledger unknown as `unknown:code:<reason>` after a module inventory; snapshots read only the
latched set (no inventory while the gateway is measured).

Preflight: bookkeeping verb `quiet-observe-check` (never reserved, so the registry epoch does
not move) and `hermes kanban quiet-hold --check`: one JSON document (registry unknowns, route,
refusal reasons), exit 0 when start would be admitted apart from the lease, 3 otherwise. It
takes no lease and writes no cooldown record.
